Title: The Beating
Credit: Adapted from "The Tell-Tale Heart" by Edgar Allan Poe (1843)
Author: akuko

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - HALLWAY - NIGHT

A long dark hallway. A shuttered lantern glows faintly in the hand of THE NARRATOR at the far end. Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it — oh so gently!

I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, so that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR opens the lantern a crack. I undid the lantern cautiously — oh, so cautiously — just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

THE OLD MAN lies sleeping in the darkness. It was open — wide, wide open — and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

The old man bolts upright in terror. I heard him sit up in the bed, crying out — "Who's there?"

The lantern drops into darkness. In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - LATER

The narrator kneels alone on the floorboards. I took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAWN

Dawn light filters through the shutters. There entered three men, who introduced themselves, with perfect suavity, as officers of the police.

The narrator brings chairs into the room with unhurried ease. I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

A bead of cold sweat trickles down his pale temple. It was a low, dull, quick sound — much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

He leaps from his chair, screaming and tearing at the floor. "Villains!" I shrieked, "dissemble no more! I admit the deed! — tear up the planks! here, here! — it is the beating of his hideous heart!"
